{
  "description": "an on_key handler firing is itself one charged instruction, exactly as every other handler kind is: four key presses on an empty-bodied handler exhaust an instruction budget of 9 and raise ol-limit. The body is empty precisely so the firing is the ONLY thing that can be charged - an implementation that charged bodies but not firings completes this program cleanly (registration + the wait statement + its four now-charged ticks = 6, well inside 9), so the fixture flips on the rule it names. The budget was 5 before issue #953 made each wait tick a charged instruction; it was re-derived to 9 so the run folds to the byte-identical event stream and refuses the same fourth firing, at the same source span",
